But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Bad thoughts come from the heart. Like murder, adultery, and other sexual sins. And also theft, false testimony, and lying to people.
Here are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus walked from Galilee and walked to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and pleaded, "Lord, son of David! Have mercy on me! My daughter is suffering terribly, possessed by a demon."
He didn't say a single word. His students came to him. They asked him, "Expel her. She's crying behind us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel. They are like a flock of sheep that has wandered off."
And then the woman fell to her knees in front of him. "Sir! Help me!" she said.
Answer, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to their dogs."
"Well, sir," she said. "But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table."
Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be to you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus walked from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds gathered around him. They brought him blind people and those who couldn't walk. They even brought people who were crippled, those who couldn't speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak began to speak. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk began to walk. Those who were blind began to see. And that made the people praise the God of Israel.
After this, Jesus called his disciples and said, "I have compassion for these people; they have already been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, for they might faint on the way."
His disciples answered him. "There is nothing here," they said. "Where will we find bread to feed this large crowd?"
Jesus asked, "How many loaves do you have?" They answered, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus told the assembly to sit on the ground.
Take the seven loaves of bread and the fish, and give thanks. Then divide them and give them to the disciples. And the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. Afterward, the disciples gathered seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was 4,000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the people away, he got into the boat. He went to the region near Magadan.
They asked about Jesus for a miraculous sign.
Then the Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
Answer, "In the evening it rises into the sky. It says, 'It will be beautiful tomorrow. The sky is red.'
In the morning you say, "Today will be stormy. The sky is red and cloudy." You understand the meaning of what you see in the sky. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign, but no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to bring bread with them.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students discussed this among themselves. They said, "He must be talking like that because we didn't bring the bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said to them, "Your faith is too small! Why are you discussing among yourselves that there is no bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Aren't you thinking about the five loaves of 5000? Aren't you thinking about how many baskets of pieces I collected?
Don't you remember the seven loaves of bread that fed 4,000? Don't you remember how many baskets of leftovers you collected?
How can you not understand? I wasn't talking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
After the disciples understood that Jesus was not speaking about them to warn them about the leaven used in bread. He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees were teaching. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus walked to the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; and still others say Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"But what's on your head?" he asked. "Who are you saying I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon, son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but My Father who is in heaven.
This is the one I was telling you about. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to tear it down.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What is bound on earth will be bound in heaven. And what is released on earth will be released in heaven.
Later, Jesus called his disciples and told them not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus was talking about his upcoming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what was going to happen to him. He told them he must go to Jerusalem. There he must suffer greatly at the hands of the elders, the leading priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed, and on the third day he must rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord! This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. For you are not thinking about the things of God, but rather about the things of people."
Later, Jesus called his disciples to him. He said to them, "If anyone wants to become my follower, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me.
He who wants to save his life will lose it. But he who loses his life for me will find it.
What good is it if someone gains the whole world but loses their own soul? Or what can a person give in exchange for their own soul?
The Son of Man is coming in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will repay each one according to his deeds.
What I am going to tell you is true. Some of the people standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus's appearance changed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John his brother James with him. He brought them up a high mountain. They were by themselves.
Under their eyes, his appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. Around him, they became brilliant like light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Master, it is good for us to stay here. If you wish, I will make three tents. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as they continued talking, a bright cloud appeared beside them. A voice from the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to Him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were very afraid. They fell face down on the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them. He said to them, "Arise, do not be afraid."
They went upstairs and didn't see anyone except Issa.
They came down the mountain. On the way down, Jesus told them what to do. "Don't tell anyone what you have seen," he said. "Be patient until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
The disciples asked, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and renew everything again."
But I tell you, Elijah has come already. The people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will also let the Son of Man suffer.
After the young men understood that Jesus was talking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As he approached the crowd, a man came to Jesus, knelt before him, and said,
"Oh Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He shakes his head a lot and suffers a lot. He often falls into the fire or water."
I brought it to your students. But they couldn't cure it.
"O people, you do not believe and are evil!" Jesus answered. "How long must I be with you? How long must I bear with you? Bring the child here to me."
Jesus ordered the evil spirit to come out of the boy, and it left him. He was healed at that very moment.
After this, the disciples came to Jesus alone. They asked him, "Why could we not drive out the demon?"
Answer, "Because you have so little faith. What I am about to tell you is true. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there.' And it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus told them, "The Son of Man will be betrayed into the hands of men."
They will kill him. On the third day he will rise from the dead." After this, the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax
Jesus and His disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, a tax collector came to Peter. They asked him, "Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?"
"Ay, what He do," the other answered. When Peter got inside the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" He asked. "Whose sons do the kings of the earth collect taxes and duties from? Do they collect it from their own sons or from other people?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children need not pay," Jesus told him.
But we don't want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find the exact coin you need inside. Take it and give it to them for my tax and yours.
